  <static seed="241" source="csafranski/5.2Preview.pg">
    <statement><p>Consider the function <m>A</m> defined by the rule
<me>A(x) = \int_1^x f(t) \, dt,</me>
where <m>f(t) = 4-2t</m>.</p>
<p>a. Compute <m>A(1)</m> and <m>A(2)</m> exactly.</p>
<p><m>A(1)=</m> <fillin characters="7" name="AnSwEr0001"/> <m>A(2)=</m> <fillin characters="7" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>b. Use the First Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to find an equivalent formula for 
<m>A(x)</m> that does not involve integrals.  That is, use the first FTC to evaluate 
<m>\int_1^x (4-2t) \, dt</m>.</p>
<p><m>A(x)=</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p>c. Observe that <m>f</m> is a linear function; what kind of function is <m>A</m>?</p>
<p><ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0004"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>Constant</p>
</li><li>
<p>Linear</p>
</li><li>
<p>Quadratic</p>
</li><li>
<p>Cubic</p>
</li><li>
<p>Trigonometric</p>
</li><li>
<p>Non-Algebraic</p>
</li></ul></p>
<p>d. Using the formula you found in (b) that does not involve integrals, compute <m>A'(x)</m>.</p>
<p><m>A'(x)=</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0005"/></p>
<p>e. While we have defined <m>f</m> by the rule <m>f(t) = 4-2t</m>, it is equivalent to say that 
<m>f</m> is given by the rule <m>f(x) = 4 - 2x</m>.  What do you observe about the relationship 
between <m>A</m> and <m>f</m>?</p>
<p><ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0006"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>A is an antiderivative of f</p>
</li><li>
<p>A is a derivative of f</p>
</li><li>
<p>A is a multiple of f</p>
</li><li>
<p>No relation</p>
</li></ul></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4\cdot 2-2^{2}-\left(4\cdot 1-1^{2}\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4x-x^{2}-\left(4\cdot 1-1^{2}\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{Quadratic}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4-2x</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{A is an antiderivative of f}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>a. We were given that <me>A(x) = \int_1^x f(t) \, dt,</me> so  <m>A(1) = \int_1^1 f(t) \, dt =0.</m></p>
<p>Then <m>A(2) = \int_1^2 f(t) \, dt = \int_1^2 (4-2t)\, dt = 4t-t^2 \bigg\vert_1^2 = (8-4)-(4-1) = 1</m></p>
<p>b.  <m>A(x) = \int_1^x f(t) \, dt =  \int_1^x (4-2t)\, dt = 4t-t^2 \bigg\vert_1^x = (4x-x^2)-(4-1) = 4x-x^{2}-3</m></p>
<p>c. <m>4x-x^{2}-3</m> is a quadratic function.</p>
<p>d. The derivative of <m>4x-x^{2}-3</m> is <m>4-2x</m>.</p>
<p>e. Thus, the derivative of <m>A</m> is <m>f</m>, or <m>A'=f</m>, so <m>A</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f</m>.</p>
</solution>
